---
export const prerender = true;
import EditorialArticleLayout from '../../components/EditorialArticleLayout.astro';

const slug = 'remove-ai-writing-tells-before-publishing';
const title = 'How to Edit an AI-Assisted Draft Before Publishing';
const description =
  'Check an AI-assisted draft for accurate facts, useful examples, clear sentences, accessible headings, and honest authorship. Includes six practical editing passes.';
const imagePath = '/social/remove-ai-writing-tells-before-publishing.webp';
const socialImagePath = '/social/remove-ai-writing-tells-before-publishing.png';
const imageAlt =
  'Full-body smoke-kawaii girl editing draft cards beside a laptop, source pages, magnifying glass, headphones, red pencil, and a checked browser preview.';
const sources = [
  {
    href: 'https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content',
    label: 'Google: creating helpful, reliable, people-first content',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/using-gen-ai-content',
    label: 'Google: using generative AI content on a website',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/hardikpandya/stop-slop/blob/main/SKILL.md',
    label: 'Stop Slop: writing rules and five subjective ratings',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02819',
    label: '2023 study: GPT detectors and non-native English writers',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.w3.org/WAI/tips/writing/',
    label: 'W3C: writing for web accessibility',
  },
];
const relatedLinks = [
  {
    href: '/tools/text-to-speech-audiobook-generator/',
    label: 'Text to Speech MP3 Generator',
    description: 'Listen to a draft with a browser voice and download an MP3 for an editing pass.',
  },
  {
    href: '/tools/reading-level-checker/',
    label: 'Reading Level Checker',
    description: 'Find long English sentences and reading estimates, then decide what needs editing.',
  },
  {
    href: '/tools/keyword-extractor/',
    label: 'Keyword Extractor',
    description: 'See repeated words and filtered phrases without treating their counts as search demand.',
  },
  {
    href: '/blog/free-ai-skills-open-source-tools-organic-growth/',
    label: 'AI skills and open-source tools for practical work',
    description: 'Compare research, writing, design, and code tools with their costs and limits.',
  },
];
---

<EditorialArticleLayout
  slug={slug}
  title={title}
  seoTitle="How to Edit an AI-Assisted Draft Before Publishing"
  description={description}
  label="Practical editing guide"
  breadcrumbLabel="Editing AI-assisted writing before publishing"
  imagePath={imagePath}
  socialImagePath={socialImagePath}
  imageAlt={imageAlt}
  imageCaption="The smoke-kawaii mascot checks a draft against sources, listens to the result, and reviews the finished browser page."
  sources={sources}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      An AI-assisted draft can sound finished while leaving a reader unsure what to do. A tool
      description might promise clear results without showing the input, the answer, or the limit
      that changes how someone should use it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Edit those gaps before polishing the tone. This guide uses Access Free Tools examples to
      show how to check claims, shorten an instruction, listen for awkward sentences, and review
      the page a reader will actually see.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Short answer</h2>
    <p>
      Start with the reader's task. Check factual claims against primary sources or a reproducible
      test, label opinions and illustrations, and remove experience you cannot support. Then edit
      sentences, headings, links, and examples. A smooth voice or a high style score cannot prove
      that the article is accurate, original, or written by a particular person.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep accurate authorship and AI-assistance disclosures with the finished page. Making a
      draft useful does not require a claim that it can pass an AI detector.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Six editing passes</h2>
    <ol class="editorial-checklist">
      <li><strong>Name the task:</strong> State what the reader will calculate, compare, fix, or decide. Cut an opening that delays that task.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the facts:</strong> Open the primary source, record the check date, and note what it supports or leaves uncertain.</li>
      <li><strong>Show a usable example:</strong> Include the input and result. Label an invented illustration and keep test conditions with a measured result.</li>
      <li><strong>Read or listen aloud:</strong> Look for repeated openings, missing words, and sentences that crowd several instructions together.</li>
      <li><strong>Use mechanical checks:</strong> Review style warnings, reading estimates, broken links, and page layout. Investigate a warning before accepting a suggested rewrite.</li>
      <li><strong>Remove empty paragraphs:</strong> Keep the detail that helps someone use this page. Delete a paragraph if it adds no fact, example, limit, or next step.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>A style score cannot check the evidence</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: <a href="https://github.com/hardikpandya/stop-slop/blob/main/SKILL.md" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Stop Slop's rules and scoring guide</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Stop Slop asks a reviewer to rate directness, rhythm, trust, authenticity, and density from
      1 to 10. Its total is a subjective writing assessment out of 50. The authenticity category
      asks how the prose sounds. It does not identify who wrote it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Access Free Tools also has automated wording and structure checks. Those checks can flag
      known phrases or long sentences, but they do not perform the same judgment as a person
      reading the article. Neither kind of score verifies a source, an experiment, or an original
      observation. Check those separately, even if the draft receives 50 out of 50.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Replace a broad benefit with an instruction</h2>
    <p>
      Consider this illustrative tool description: “Browser text to speech provides a practical
      way to create audio.” It names a category but leaves the reader without a step to try.
    </p>
    <p>
      A more useful instruction is: “Paste your draft into the text box and choose a voice.
      Generate the audio, then listen for repeated openings and crowded sentences.” You can follow
      that sequence in the <a href="/tools/text-to-speech-audiobook-generator/">Text to Speech MP3 Generator</a>.
      Allow time for the model download on a first run. Loading speed depends on the device,
      connection, model, and cache.
    </p>
    <p>
      Use an exact loading time only when you have the run record and its conditions. A precise
      number without those details adds confidence the evidence has not earned. If no record is
      available, describe the limitation without inventing a test or a personal scene.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Check a long sentence with the actual tool</h2>
    <p>
      Paste this example into the <a href="/tools/reading-level-checker/">Reading Level Checker</a>:
      “Paste your draft into the text box and choose a voice before you generate the audio so you
      can listen for repeated openings and crowded sentences.”
    </p>
    <p>
      The checker counts 26 words in that sentence and flags 1 sentence over 20 words. The revised
      instruction in the previous section has 22 words across 2 sentences, with 11 words in each.
      It produces no sentence-over-20 flag and separates setup from listening.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a clue to review the instruction, rather than proof that the shorter version is
      right for every reader. The tool estimates English syllables for its reading scores. Names,
      abbreviations, numbers, and mixed-language passages can distort those estimates. Keep a
      needed technical term and explain it instead of chasing the lowest grade.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Listen, then check the page structure</h2>
    <p>
      A speech voice can make repeated sentence openings or a missing word easier to notice.
      It cannot judge whether the facts are true. Listen to the awkward passage, edit it, and
      compare the meaning with the original before accepting the change.
    </p>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/tips/writing/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">W3C writing guidance</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      W3C recommends descriptive headings, meaningful link text, and clear, concise content.
      Use actual heading elements, such as <code>h2</code> and <code>h3</code>, in a logical
      hierarchy. Making text large or bold alone does not give assistive technology a heading it
      can use for navigation. Check the published page's structure as well as the words in the editor.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Use repeated words to review the subject</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/keyword-extractor/">Keyword Extractor</a> counts repeated words and
      short phrases after removing common words. Use the list to spot accidental repetition or
      a product name that dominates a draft about something else. Filtering can join words that
      were not adjacent in the original, so check a phrase against the draft before quoting it.
    </p>
    <p>
      These counts do not measure search demand or predict a ranking. Write for the task first.
      Google's guidance allows useful AI-assisted research and structure, while warning that
      generating many pages without added value may violate its scaled-content spam policy.
      It also calls for factual review of generated text, titles, descriptions, and structured data.
    </p>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Sources: Google's <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/using-gen-ai-content" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">generative AI content guidance</a>
      and <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">people-first content guidance</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Do not use a detector score as authorship proof</h2>
    <p class="article-source-note">
      Source: <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02819" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">the 2023 study of GPT-detector bias</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The researchers tested several detectors on native and non-native English writing samples
      in 2023. They found that the tested detectors often misclassified non-native English writing
      as AI-generated. That finding applies to the study's samples and detectors. It does not
      establish how every current detector performs.
    </p>
    <p>
      A detector percentage cannot replace evidence of authorship. Keep draft history, sources,
      and relevant test records. Avoid adding deliberate mistakes or promising an “undetectable”
      rewrite. Those promises do not tell a reader whether the article is useful or true.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section article-flow-section">
    <h2>Review the final copy and its disclosures</h2>
    <ul class="editorial-checklist">
      <li>Can the reader follow the example without opening another page?</li>
      <li>Do measured results include their inputs and test conditions?</li>
      <li>Does each important claim have a source that supports that wording?</li>
      <li>Are opinions, illustrations, and uncertainty clear?</li>
      <li>Do the title, summary, and structured data match the visible article?</li>
      <li>Do links describe their destinations and headings convey the page structure?</li>
      <li>Does the page accurately explain authorship, ownership, and AI assistance?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If pasted text contains an unexpected invisible character, inspect the affected text and
      its meaning before removing it. That is a text-formatting check. It gives no reason to
      strip image provenance, copyright information, attribution, or AI disclosures.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with one paragraph and keep its meaning intact while editing it. Use the
      <a href="/tools/reading-level-checker/">Reading Level Checker</a> and its
      <a href="/blog/how-to-use-reading-level-checker/">reading-level guide</a> to find a sentence
      worth reviewing, then decide whether the change helps the reader finish the task.
    </p>
  </section>
</EditorialArticleLayout>
